1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,244
You install a fresh Ubuntu or Debian server.

2
00:00:03,244 --> 00:00:08,170
Out of the box, it accepts password logins, and it lets root log in with a key.

3
00:00:08,733 --> 00:00:14,983
Bots start guessing SSH passwords on internet facing servers within minutes of them coming online.

4
00:00:15,533 --> 00:00:18,833
And when teams harden servers by hand from a checklist,

5
00:00:18,833 --> 00:00:25,613
every server ends up a little different, and nobody notices when someone quietly switches password login back on.

6
00:00:26,167 --> 00:00:31,457
In this video I will show you a project that fixes all of that with one Ansible command:

7
00:00:31,457 --> 00:00:36,207
the same security baseline on every server, with monitoring and an audit score.

8
00:00:36,767 --> 00:00:38,296
And everything is measured.

9
00:00:38,296 --> 00:00:40,846
The Lynis hardening score goes from 61 to 77,

10
00:00:40,846 --> 00:00:45,604
password logins are rejected, and a brute force attacker is banned in seven seconds.

11
00:00:45,604 --> 00:00:49,117
The code and a free study guide are linked in the description.

12
00:00:50,867 --> 00:00:52,164
Here is the plan.

13
00:00:52,164 --> 00:00:57,277
First, why a fresh server is not safe, and the idea behind the fix.

14
00:00:57,833 --> 00:01:00,649
Then the architecture, and the lab:

15
00:01:00,649 --> 00:01:05,233
a controller, two servers and an attacker, all in Docker.

16
00:01:05,767 --> 00:01:07,868
Then the roles, one by one:

17
00:01:07,868 --> 00:01:14,017
SSH, the firewall, fail2ban, the kernel, automatic updates and the audit trail.

18
00:01:14,567 --> 00:01:18,945
Next, the safety nets that make sure the automation can never lock you out,

19
00:01:18,945 --> 00:01:22,097
and the monitoring with Prometheus, Grafana and Lynis.

20
00:01:22,633 --> 00:01:25,275
And finally the tests that prove it on every push,

21
00:01:25,275 --> 00:01:29,713
how to run the whole lab on your own laptop, and how to roll it out to real servers.

22
00:01:31,467 --> 00:01:33,511
Let us start with the problem.

23
00:01:33,511 --> 00:01:40,937
Debian and Ubuntu install OpenSSH with password logins switched on, and they allow root to log in with a key.

24
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:45,056
The firewall is open, so every port a service opens is reachable.

25
00:01:45,056 --> 00:01:47,080
And nothing records who changed what.

26
00:01:47,633 --> 00:01:50,706
Hardening is usually done by hand, from a checklist.

27
00:01:50,706 --> 00:01:54,843
So ten servers hardened by hand become ten slightly different servers.

28
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:56,527
Then drift sets in.

29
00:01:56,527 --> 00:02:02,580
Someone temporarily switches password login back on to debug something, and it stays that way forever.

30
00:02:03,133 --> 00:02:05,071
And hardening itself is risky.

31
00:02:05,071 --> 00:02:11,853
One typo in the SSH config or one wrong firewall rule, and you have cut off the only way into the server.

32
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:13,983
Finally, if someone asks:

33
00:02:13,983 --> 00:02:15,377
is this server secure?

34
00:02:15,377 --> 00:02:20,760
Without an audit score, there is no number to show, and no way to catch a regression.

35
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:23,555
Here is the idea:

36
00:02:23,555 --> 00:02:30,130
write the security baseline as code, with Ansible, and apply it to any number of servers with one command.

37
00:02:30,667 --> 00:02:32,058
Every change must be safe.

38
00:02:32,058 --> 00:02:36,608
Risky files are validated by the program that will read them before they are written,

39
00:02:36,608 --> 00:02:39,767
and guards stop the run before anything could lock you out.

40
00:02:40,333 --> 00:02:41,779
The playbook is idempotent:

41
00:02:41,779 --> 00:02:43,545
running it again changes nothing.

42
00:02:43,545 --> 00:02:45,419
That gives you two things for free.

43
00:02:45,419 --> 00:02:47,667
In check mode, a re-run is a drift report.

44
00:02:47,667 --> 00:02:49,433
In normal mode, it is the repair.

45
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:57,310
The result is measured with Lynis, an open source audit tool, before and after, and then every week.

46
00:02:57,867 --> 00:03:00,249
And the protections themselves are monitored,

47
00:03:00,249 --> 00:03:04,007
with alerts, and proven by tests that attack the servers on every push.

48
00:03:05,767 --> 00:03:07,332
Here is the architecture.

49
00:03:07,332 --> 00:03:11,527
You run one command, make harden, or the Ansible playbook directly.

50
00:03:12,067 --> 00:03:18,316
A controller container runs Ansible, and connects to the servers over SSH, with a key.

51
00:03:18,316 --> 00:03:20,714
In the lab there are two servers:

52
00:03:20,714 --> 00:03:24,057
web-01 on Ubuntu 24.04 and db-01 on Debian 12.

53
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:27,425
An attacker container sits on the same network.

54
00:03:27,425 --> 00:03:30,610
The tests use it for SSH brute force and port probes.

55
00:03:31,167 --> 00:03:36,769
Prometheus scrapes every server's node exporter on port 9100 every fifteen seconds,

56
00:03:36,769 --> 00:03:38,862
and evaluates nine alert rules.

57
00:03:38,862 --> 00:03:41,427
Grafana shows it all on one dashboard.

58
00:03:41,967 --> 00:03:45,604
After the run, every server has the same set of protections:

59
00:03:45,604 --> 00:03:48,333
hardened SSH, an nftables firewall, fail2ban,

60
00:03:48,333 --> 00:03:53,547
automatic updates, kernel settings, an audit trail, metrics, and a weekly Lynis audit.

61
00:03:55,300 --> 00:03:57,855
Ansible is an agentless configuration tool.

62
00:03:57,855 --> 00:04:02,965
It connects over SSH, and its modules only change what differs from the desired state.

63
00:04:02,965 --> 00:04:04,510
This is the main playbook.

64
00:04:05,067 --> 00:04:11,730
Serial one means one server at a time, and max fail percentage zero means the first failure stops the rollout.

65
00:04:11,730 --> 00:04:14,577
A mistake hits one server, not the whole fleet.

66
00:04:15,133 --> 00:04:17,346
Then nine roles, in a fixed order.

67
00:04:17,346 --> 00:04:23,723
Each security control is its own role, with its own defaults, templates, and its own verify tasks.

68
00:04:24,267 --> 00:04:27,360
At the end, it applies pending service changes,

69
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:31,771
throws away the existing SSH connection, and opens a brand new one.

70
00:04:31,771 --> 00:04:37,037
If the new SSH or firewall settings had locked us out, the run fails right here.

71
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:41,890
Role one, common, creates the administrators.

72
00:04:41,890 --> 00:04:46,215
They log in with SSH keys only, and any other keys are removed.

73
00:04:46,215 --> 00:04:52,600
It also sets up sudo, login banners, a strict umask, no core dumps, and a persistent journal.

74
00:04:53,167 --> 00:04:55,320
This role has the first safety net.

75
00:04:55,320 --> 00:05:01,287
If no administrator with an SSH key is defined, the run stops right there, before SSH is touched.

76
00:05:01,833 --> 00:05:03,701
Role two hardens the kernel:

77
00:05:03,701 --> 00:05:09,971
34 sysctl settings, for example ignoring ICMP redirects, persisted to a file and applied live.

78
00:05:09,971 --> 00:05:13,173
And nine rarely used kernel modules are blocked.

79
00:05:13,733 --> 00:05:14,829
One design detail:

80
00:05:14,829 --> 00:05:19,575
the role reads the running values first, and writes only the ones that differ.

81
00:05:19,575 --> 00:05:23,773
So when this role reports changed, it really means something drifted.

82
00:05:25,533 --> 00:05:26,634
Role three is SSH.

83
00:05:26,634 --> 00:05:29,508
Only members of the ssh users group may log in,

84
00:05:29,508 --> 00:05:33,667
root may never log in, and the only accepted method is a public key.

85
00:05:33,667 --> 00:05:36,663
Password and keyboard interactive logins are off.

86
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:42,890
Tunnels and forwarding are switched off, so a login is a shell, not a network path into the server.

87
00:05:43,433 --> 00:05:46,490
Only modern key exchange, ciphers and MACs are allowed,

88
00:05:46,490 --> 00:05:52,213
weak Diffie Hellman groups are removed, and verbose logging records the key fingerprint of every login.

89
00:05:52,767 --> 00:05:54,153
Two details matter.

90
00:05:54,153 --> 00:06:00,136
The file is a drop in named zero zero, because sshd uses the first value it reads,

91
00:06:00,136 --> 00:06:02,544
so cloud init cannot override it.

92
00:06:02,544 --> 00:06:06,338
And validate runs sshd dash t on the new file first.

93
00:06:06,338 --> 00:06:09,987
If the check fails, the old config stays in place.

94
00:06:11,733 --> 00:06:15,784
Role four is the firewall, with nftables, the modern Linux firewall.

95
00:06:15,784 --> 00:06:18,465
The input chain has a default policy of drop:

96
00:06:18,465 --> 00:06:21,503
anything that is not explicitly allowed is dropped.

97
00:06:22,067 --> 00:06:25,100
Then one rule per allowed port and source.

98
00:06:25,100 --> 00:06:30,877
For example, port 9100, the metrics port, is only open to the Prometheus server.

99
00:06:31,433 --> 00:06:35,578
The project uses its own table, and never flushes the whole rule set.

100
00:06:35,578 --> 00:06:39,723
That way the tables of Docker, fail2ban or Kubernetes stay untouched.

101
00:06:40,267 --> 00:06:41,939
And again, two safety nets.

102
00:06:41,939 --> 00:06:45,967
The new rules are checked with nft dash c before they are loaded.

103
00:06:45,967 --> 00:06:51,357
And if the rule list has no rule for SSH, the run stops before the firewall is touched.

104
00:06:53,100 --> 00:06:54,552
Role five is fail2ban.

105
00:06:54,552 --> 00:06:57,983
It reads failed SSH logins from the systemd journal.

106
00:06:57,983 --> 00:07:03,460
In aggressive mode it also counts scanners that never even try a key or a password.

107
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:10,740
After too many failures, the address is banned, in fail2ban's own nftables table, next to the baseline table.

108
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:14,660
Repeat offenders get longer and longer bans, up to one week.

109
00:07:15,200 --> 00:07:20,376
And the Ansible controller is on the ignore list, so a ban can never cut off your automation.

110
00:07:20,376 --> 00:07:23,660
A small timer also exports the ban counters for Prometheus.

111
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:29,370
Role six turns on daily security updates with unattended upgrades.

112
00:07:29,370 --> 00:07:31,595
Reboots are not automatic by default:

113
00:07:31,595 --> 00:07:33,400
a reboot should be a decision.

114
00:07:33,967 --> 00:07:37,128
Role seven is auditd, the kernel audit trail.

115
00:07:37,128 --> 00:07:40,991
It records changes to accounts, sudo, the SSH settings,

116
00:07:40,991 --> 00:07:48,297
the firewall, services, the clock and kernel modules, and every command run as root by a logged in user.

117
00:07:48,833 --> 00:07:55,108
Role eight installs node exporter, pinned and checksum verified, in a sandboxed systemd unit.

118
00:07:55,108 --> 00:08:01,113
It exports the state of every security service, and custom metrics from small text files.

119
00:08:01,667 --> 00:08:06,341
And role nine installs Lynis 3.1.7, also pinned and checksum verified.

120
00:08:06,341 --> 00:08:12,951
A weekly timer runs the audit, and a small Python script turns the report into a Prometheus metric:

121
00:08:12,951 --> 00:08:14,287
the hardening index.

122
00:08:16,033 --> 00:08:21,584
Automation that changes SSH and the firewall can lock you out of every server at once.

123
00:08:21,584 --> 00:08:23,972
So this project has four safety nets.

124
00:08:23,972 --> 00:08:25,134
First, the guards:

125
00:08:25,134 --> 00:08:32,233
the run stops if no admin key exists, if the allowed SSH groups are empty, or if the firewall has no SSH rule.

126
00:08:32,800 --> 00:08:35,097
Second, validate before write.

127
00:08:35,097 --> 00:08:39,768
Every risky file is checked by the program that will read it:

128
00:08:39,768 --> 00:08:42,525
sshd dash t, nft dash c, and visudo.

129
00:08:42,525 --> 00:08:46,200
If the check fails, the old file stays in place.

130
00:08:46,767 --> 00:08:48,578
Third, one server at a time.

131
00:08:48,578 --> 00:08:52,267
If something goes wrong, it stops after the first server.

132
00:08:52,833 --> 00:08:54,954
And fourth, the access proof:

133
00:08:54,954 --> 00:09:00,513
a brand new SSH connection at the end shows that the admin can still get in.

134
00:09:02,267 --> 00:09:06,795
And the end to end test makes these mistakes on purpose.

135
00:09:06,795 --> 00:09:10,110
Here is a run with an unknown SSH cipher.

136
00:09:10,110 --> 00:09:15,447
sshd dash t rejects the file, the task fails, and changed is zero.

137
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:20,679
An invalid firewall address, three hundred dot one dot one dot one,

138
00:09:20,679 --> 00:09:24,660
is rejected by nft dash c, and the old rules stay active.

139
00:09:25,200 --> 00:09:30,870
And with no administrator defined, the lockout guard stops the run before SSH is touched.

140
00:09:31,433 --> 00:09:38,023
After all three failed runs, the admin can still log in, and a drift check confirms that nothing was changed.

141
00:09:39,767 --> 00:09:41,572
So what does the baseline change?

142
00:09:41,572 --> 00:09:44,854
Here are the measured results from the lab, on both servers.

143
00:09:44,854 --> 00:09:48,737
Before, with distribution defaults, the servers offered password login,

144
00:09:48,737 --> 00:09:52,457
accepted the correct password, and let root log in with a valid key.

145
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:56,751
After the baseline, the only login method offered is public key.

146
00:09:56,751 --> 00:10:01,028
The correct password is rejected, root is rejected even with a valid key,

147
00:10:01,028 --> 00:10:03,900
and the metrics port is blocked for the attacker.

148
00:10:04,467 --> 00:10:10,347
And the admin still logs in with a key, and Prometheus still scrapes both servers through the firewall.

149
00:10:12,100 --> 00:10:15,303
Lynis gives every server a hardening index.

150
00:10:15,303 --> 00:10:22,230
Both lab servers went from 61 to 77, and the same result came from three separate clean runs.

151
00:10:22,767 --> 00:10:26,896
On web-01, the number of Lynis suggestions dropped from 44 to 26.

152
00:10:26,896 --> 00:10:30,899
Many of the remaining ones come from the container environment,

153
00:10:30,899 --> 00:10:35,347
or from choices left to you, like separate partitions or a mail relay.

154
00:10:35,900 --> 00:10:40,294
On a real virtual machine in CI, the score went from 61 to 73.

155
00:10:40,294 --> 00:10:43,271
A score is a direction, not a certificate,

156
00:10:43,271 --> 00:10:48,870
but it shows progress, and an alert fires if it ever drops below 75 in the lab.

157
00:10:50,633 --> 00:10:56,853
The first run made 94 changes in 169 seconds, one server at a time.

158
00:10:57,400 --> 00:11:00,399
The second run made zero changes on both servers.

159
00:11:00,399 --> 00:11:04,500
That is idempotence, and it is what makes drift detection possible.

160
00:11:05,033 --> 00:11:09,121
Then the verify playbook checks every single control on both servers.

161
00:11:09,121 --> 00:11:10,543
It passed in 26 seconds.

162
00:11:12,300 --> 00:11:13,357
Now the attack.

163
00:11:13,357 --> 00:11:18,218
The attacker container tries to log in to db-01 with wrong passwords.

164
00:11:18,218 --> 00:11:24,840
It was banned after two failed logins, because in aggressive mode one connection counts twice.

165
00:11:25,400 --> 00:11:28,517
The ban came seven seconds after the first attempt.

166
00:11:28,517 --> 00:11:31,940
Across the runs, it was between three and seven seconds.

167
00:11:32,500 --> 00:11:35,687
From then on, SSH from that address is blocked.

168
00:11:35,687 --> 00:11:40,840
But the Ansible controller still connects, because it is on the ignore list.

169
00:11:41,400 --> 00:11:46,889
And the SSH attacker banned alert fired in Prometheus 100 seconds after the attack started.

170
00:11:46,889 --> 00:11:51,233
That delay depends on the one minute timer that exports the ban counter.

171
00:11:51,233 --> 00:11:52,620
The ban lasts one hour.

172
00:11:54,367 --> 00:11:55,505
What about drift?

173
00:11:55,505 --> 00:11:58,651
The test makes three changes by hand on web-01:

174
00:11:58,651 --> 00:12:04,007
password login back on, the firewall table deleted, and ICMP redirects accepted.

175
00:12:04,567 --> 00:12:06,133
Then it runs the drift check.

176
00:12:06,133 --> 00:12:10,077
That is the same playbook, in check and diff mode, so it changes nothing.

177
00:12:10,077 --> 00:12:11,374
It shows the exact line:

178
00:12:11,374 --> 00:12:13,697
password authentication yes, instead of no.

179
00:12:14,233 --> 00:12:17,178
It lists exactly the three settings that drifted,

180
00:12:17,178 --> 00:12:21,145
and exits with code two, so a scheduler or CI job can alert on it.

181
00:12:21,145 --> 00:12:25,833
And because check mode is read only, the firewall is still deleted afterwards.

182
00:12:26,367 --> 00:12:29,911
Running the playbook again on web-01 restored everything:

183
00:12:29,911 --> 00:12:31,590
five changes in 51 seconds.

184
00:12:31,590 --> 00:12:33,767
And the next drift check was clean.

185
00:12:35,533 --> 00:12:39,840
Hardening is not a one time event, so the protections are monitored.

186
00:12:39,840 --> 00:12:45,983
This is the Grafana dashboard, provisioned from a JSON file in git, at the end of a real lab run.

187
00:12:46,533 --> 00:12:49,958
It shows the Lynis score per server, banned addresses,

188
00:12:49,958 --> 00:12:52,558
firing alerts, days since the last audit,

189
00:12:52,558 --> 00:12:59,153
every security service per server, the baseline version, and fail2ban activity, CPU, memory and network.

190
00:13:00,900 --> 00:13:03,609
Prometheus evaluates nine alert rules.

191
00:13:03,609 --> 00:13:05,890
Server down covers availability.

192
00:13:06,433 --> 00:13:09,058
Three rules watch the protections themselves:

193
00:13:09,058 --> 00:13:13,723
a security service that stopped, auditd not running, and fail2ban not answering.

194
00:13:14,267 --> 00:13:15,846
Two rules watch the audit:

195
00:13:15,846 --> 00:13:22,287
the score dropping below 75, and a report older than eight days, which means the weekly timer did not run.

196
00:13:22,833 --> 00:13:25,318
And the rest cover attacks and housekeeping.

197
00:13:25,318 --> 00:13:28,593
Every rule has a unit test that runs with prom tool in CI.

198
00:13:30,333 --> 00:13:34,620
All of this runs in GitHub Actions on every push, in four layers.

199
00:13:34,620 --> 00:13:36,005
First, static checks:

200
00:13:36,005 --> 00:13:43,193
yamllint, ansible lint with the production profile, ShellCheck, seven pytest tests, and the alert rule tests.

201
00:13:43,733 --> 00:13:45,172
Second, Molecule.

202
00:13:45,172 --> 00:13:53,970
It runs every role on three distributions, Ubuntu 24.04, Debian 12 and Debian 13, in systemd containers.

203
00:13:53,970 --> 00:13:59,638
The second run must change nothing, and 44 verify checks must pass.

204
00:13:59,638 --> 00:14:03,783
Locally the whole Molecule test took 430 seconds.

205
00:14:04,333 --> 00:14:08,695
Third, the lab end to end test, with real SSH and real attacks:

206
00:14:08,695 --> 00:14:11,050
everything you saw in the results.

207
00:14:11,050 --> 00:14:15,273
The final clean run took 823 seconds, and every check passed.

208
00:14:15,833 --> 00:14:18,381
And fourth, a real virtual machine.

209
00:14:18,381 --> 00:14:22,675
Containers cannot load audit rules or most kernel settings,

210
00:14:22,675 --> 00:14:26,605
so a CI job applies the baseline to the runner itself:

211
00:14:26,605 --> 00:14:34,393
50 changes in 121 seconds, then zero changes, with all 34 kernel settings live and every audit rule loaded.

212
00:14:36,133 --> 00:14:38,108
And the tests found real bugs.

213
00:14:38,108 --> 00:14:44,429
The unit tests caught that the metric writer printed the Lynis timestamp in scientific notation,

214
00:14:44,429 --> 00:14:45,548
losing precision.

215
00:14:45,548 --> 00:14:48,313
Whole numbers are now written as integers.

216
00:14:48,867 --> 00:14:53,730
Molecule found that the Lynis download site answers 403 to Ansible.

217
00:14:53,730 --> 00:14:59,827
The project now downloads a pinned GitHub release, verified by its SHA 256 checksum.

218
00:15:00,367 --> 00:15:04,164
The end to end test found that its own unban check,

219
00:15:04,164 --> 00:15:08,705
which sent an SSH probe, was counted as another failed login.

220
00:15:08,705 --> 00:15:11,907
It now checks the nftables ban set instead.

221
00:15:12,467 --> 00:15:13,769
And one honest limit.

222
00:15:13,769 --> 00:15:18,171
On GitHub hosted runners, auditd was running and every rule was loaded,

223
00:15:18,171 --> 00:15:22,016
but the kernel produced no audit records at all, for any rule.

224
00:15:22,016 --> 00:15:24,806
The test reports this as not verifiable here,

225
00:15:24,806 --> 00:15:29,147
instead of hiding it, and you should confirm it once on a real server.

226
00:15:30,900 --> 00:15:32,661
Now let us make it practical.

227
00:15:32,661 --> 00:15:35,698
You need Docker with Compose, and a Bash terminal.

228
00:15:35,698 --> 00:15:39,768
Git Bash on Windows works, and about three gigabytes of disk space.

229
00:15:39,768 --> 00:15:42,440
Clone the repository and go into the folder.

230
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:48,466
Make lab up creates two fresh servers, web-01 on Ubuntu and db-01 on Debian,

231
00:15:48,466 --> 00:15:51,487
plus the attacker, Prometheus and Grafana.

232
00:15:51,487 --> 00:15:56,881
Ansible, Molecule and the linters are all inside a pinned controller image,

233
00:15:56,881 --> 00:15:59,470
so you do not install anything else.

234
00:16:00,033 --> 00:16:05,793
Then measure the untouched servers with make audit, apply the baseline with make harden, and measure again.

235
00:16:06,333 --> 00:16:09,289
Make compare shows the before and after table,

236
00:16:09,289 --> 00:16:15,393
make verify checks every control, and make drift shows anything that differs from the baseline.

237
00:16:15,933 --> 00:16:20,703
Grafana runs on localhost port 3000, and Prometheus on port 9090.

238
00:16:20,703 --> 00:16:25,693
To run the whole proof from scratch, with the attacks, run make e2e.

239
00:16:25,693 --> 00:16:30,903
It takes about ten minutes, and make lab down removes everything again.

240
00:16:32,667 --> 00:16:36,056
Once the lab is up, practice like an attacker and like an operator.

241
00:16:36,056 --> 00:16:38,637
Try to log in with a password, and see it rejected.

242
00:16:39,200 --> 00:16:45,510
Run a brute force from the attacker container, and watch fail2ban ban it, and the alert appear in Prometheus.

243
00:16:46,067 --> 00:16:52,077
Change a setting by hand on one of the servers, then let make drift find it, and make harden repair it.

244
00:16:52,633 --> 00:16:57,060
And make a mistake on purpose, like an invalid cipher or firewall address,

245
00:16:57,060 --> 00:16:59,273
and see the safety nets stop the run.

246
00:16:59,273 --> 00:17:02,443
The study guide has nine hands-on labs, step by step.

247
00:17:04,200 --> 00:17:06,356
How do you use this on real servers?

248
00:17:06,356 --> 00:17:11,268
Copy the lab inventory to a new folder for your environment, and set three things:

249
00:17:11,268 --> 00:17:16,120
the SSH user, your administrators with their public keys, and the firewall rules.

250
00:17:16,667 --> 00:17:17,546
Then preview.

251
00:17:17,546 --> 00:17:20,253
Run the playbook in check and diff mode.

252
00:17:20,253 --> 00:17:24,517
It changes nothing, and shows you exactly what it would change.

253
00:17:25,067 --> 00:17:25,913
Then a canary:

254
00:17:25,913 --> 00:17:30,087
apply it to one host with limit, and check that you can still log in.

255
00:17:30,633 --> 00:17:34,043
Then the rest of the fleet, one server at a time.

256
00:17:34,600 --> 00:17:35,536
And keep it true:

257
00:17:35,536 --> 00:17:40,990
run the drift check on a schedule, watch the weekly Lynis score, and route the alerts to your team.

258
00:17:42,733 --> 00:17:44,823
Let us be honest about the limits.

259
00:17:44,823 --> 00:17:46,973
It supports Debian and Ubuntu only.

260
00:17:47,533 --> 00:17:51,846
Containers and a CI virtual machine stand in for real cloud servers,

261
00:17:51,846 --> 00:17:55,841
and containers cannot load audit rules or most kernel settings.

262
00:17:55,841 --> 00:17:57,743
That is why the VM job exists.

263
00:17:58,300 --> 00:18:01,745
Lynis is a heuristic, not a compliance certificate.

264
00:18:01,745 --> 00:18:05,460
The score shows direction, not that a server is secure.

265
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:07,452
And next on the list:

266
00:18:07,452 --> 00:18:12,500
mapping every task to the CIS benchmark, alert routing with Alertmanager,

267
00:18:12,500 --> 00:18:17,410
TLS for node exporter, and SSH certificates instead of individual keys.

268
00:18:19,167 --> 00:18:20,242
That is the project.

269
00:18:20,242 --> 00:18:22,875
One command turns a fresh server into a hardened,

270
00:18:22,875 --> 00:18:26,907
monitored and audited server, and the same command finds and repairs drift.

271
00:18:27,467 --> 00:18:31,028
The code, the documentation and a free study guide,

272
00:18:31,028 --> 00:18:36,126
with nine labs and 25 interview questions, are linked in the description.

273
00:18:36,126 --> 00:18:39,967
Clone it, run make lab up, and attack your own servers.

274
00:18:40,533 --> 00:18:42,317
Now I would like to hear from you:

275
00:18:42,317 --> 00:18:44,888
how do you keep your servers on a baseline today?

276
00:18:44,888 --> 00:18:46,147
Tell me in the comments.

277
00:18:46,147 --> 00:18:49,294
And if this helped, subscribe for more real DevOps projects.

278
00:18:49,294 --> 00:18:50,343
Thanks for watching.
